   "en": {
    "headline": "OpenAI publishes its models' results in mathematics, theoretical computer science and physics",
    "summary_short": "OpenAI shares what its models have achieved on the Kakeya conjecture, the Exponential Time Hypothesis and quantum chromodynamics. It invites researchers to verify and build on the results.",
    "summary_long": "OpenAI has published results obtained by its models on three open problems. They are the Kakeya conjecture, in geometry; the Exponential Time Hypothesis, about how fast certain hard problems can be solved; and quantum chromodynamics, the theory of the strong nuclear force. The company also explains how the academic community can check them and keep working on them.\n\nOn Hacker News it was presented as “three new discoveries” and gathered more than 800 points. A paper titled “The Exponential Time Hypothesis Is False” is also circulating there. If confirmed, it would be a major result in theoretical computer science. Its validity depends on expert review.",
    "unconfirmed_claims": [
     "A paper linked on Hacker News claims that the Exponential Time Hypothesis is false."
    ]
   },
